### Playtest (playtester@90c7f8ff, `report.md`, on `playtest-1`)
No defect against R1–R8, over `file://` and a static server; won 5–0, play again, lost 0–5, click to play again → 0–0. Pause and focus-loss pause work; scaling OK at 640x400. Audio not heard (headless), and its hooks fired without errors.

### Audio (producer, inspected)
Five WAVs, mono 16-bit 22050 Hz, 0.06–1.04 s, peak ≤15838 (no clipping); played through `assets.play`, which unlocks from the start key.

### Difficulty (non-blocking; `acceptance/difficulty.py`, `delivery-1/difficulty.txt`)
The playtester's simulated players only chase the ball's current height, and they lose: "average" won 0 of 40. My simulated players anticipate where the ball will cross, and they won 40 of 40 at every reaction/noise level tried (0.2–0.45 s, ±20–50 px). So a human who reads the ball beats the computer and one who chases loses. R3 is met (beatable, and it can win). The opponent is likely easy for anyone who anticipates, but tuning is not an input requirement, so I am not holding the release for it.

